 The scans below are what the query forces, assuming Postgres and no indexes beyond primary keys.\n\n**Scans it forces**\n\n1. **Sequential scan on `users`.** `lower(u.email) = '...'` wraps the column in a function, so a plain index on `email` can't be used. The database runs `lower()` on every row to find one user. This is the most likely cause of the slowdown, and it grows with the size of `users`.\n2. **Sequential scan or full hash join on `orders`.** The join on `o.user_id = u.id` needs an index on `orders.user_id`. Without one, the database reads the whole `orders` table to find a single user's rows. Foreign keys don't get an index automatically in Postgres.\n3. **Wide row fetch from `SELECT *`.** It pulls every column from both tables, so an index-only scan is impossible. The result also has two `id` columns and similar name clashes. If `orders` has big text or JSON columns, this adds I/O on top of the scans.\n\n**Fixes**\n\n```sql\n-- 1. Make the lower(email) predicate indexable\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\n\n-- 2. Make the join side indexable\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_user_id ON orders (user_id);\n```\n\nThen rewrite the query to select only the columns the page renders:\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, o.status, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM orders o\nJOIN users u ON u.id = o.user_id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = lower('a@example.org');\n```\n\nThe columns above are placeholders, so swap in the real ones. Lowercasing the parameter on the app side or in SQL keeps the match case-insensitive.\n\n**Alternatives**\n- Store emails lowercased at write time, or use `citext`. Then you can drop `lower()` and use a plain unique index on `email`.\n- If the page lists many orders per user, add `ORDER BY o.created_at DESC LIMIT n`. An index on `(user_id, created_at DESC)` would then replace the single-column one.\n\n**Before you apply this**\n- Run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` on the current query to confirm the two seq scans. If `users` is small and the time is going elsewhere, such as a missing `orders.user_id` index or a slow app layer, the plan will show it.\n- I don't know why this regressed last week. Check whether an index was dropped, `lower()` was newly added, or a table grew sharply. I can check `git log` for the change if you want.\n\nI haven't changed any files or touched a database.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}